// Pushing a computed string into a `string[]` that is a *field of an object*
// leaked the string. The same push into a plain local `string[]` did not, that
// shape was the control for this case, and stays clean.
//
// Found by `scripts/sanitizers` only after the two `known/` categories ahead of
// it were closed: the earlier failure aborted the run in `algorithm_runtime`,
// which runs before `array_runtime`, so this had never been reached. The test
// it fails, `push_of_a_computed_string_into_an_object_field_array`, predates
// the session that found it and passes without sanitizers.
//
// Comparing the two shapes at the IR level: both emit exactly one string
// release (the push temporary, released right after the call) correct in
// both. The difference was what was missing. The plain local emitted a deep
// release of the array's contents at scope exit; the object-field version
// emitted only a shallow `free` of the buffer, so the element the array kept
// was never released. LeakSanitizer attributed the bytes to the original
// `toUpperCase` rather than to a copy, which fits: the buffer is
// reference-counted, the push shares it instead of copying, the temporary's
// release drops the count to one, and the array held the last reference.
//
// The cause was a static answer to a question that had stopped being static.
// An array literal records a release plan at construction, `Deep` when its
// elements are allocations, `Buffer` when they are `.rodata` pointers a deep
// release would corrupt. `[]` has no elements, so it recorded `Buffer`, and a
// later `push` of an owned string made that answer wrong. A plain local does
// not depend on the plan (mutating one promotes its own runtime ownership mode,
// which supersedes it); an array inside an object has no such slot, so the plan
// was the only answer it ever got.
//
// Closed by observing that the distinction is no longer needed for strings at
// all: `TyrusString::release` returns immediately for a value that does not own
// its buffer, so a deep release of a literal of static strings frees the buffer
// and touches nothing else. A `string[]` therefore records `Deep` whatever its
// elements looked like at construction, and stays correct however it is mutated
// afterwards. Nothing changed for any other element type.
interface Bucket {
    labels: string[];
}

const main = (): void => {
    let total: usize = 0;
    for (let i: i32 = 0; i < 200; i += 1) {
        let bucket: Bucket = { labels: [] };
        bucket.labels.push("key".toUpperCase());
        total += bucket.labels[0].length;
    }
    console.log(total);
    console.log("assert:", 600);
};
